  "full_response": "Here's a sample `.env` file for a Python Flask application with the requested environment variables:\n\n```env\n# Flask Configuration\nSECRET_KEY=your_very_secret_key_here_change_in_production\n\n# Database Configuration\nDATABASE_URL=postgresql://username:password@localhost:5432/dbname\n\n# AWS Configuration\nAWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=your_aws_access_key_id\nAWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=your_aws_secret_access_key\nAWS_REGION=us-east-1  # Optional, specify your AWS region\n\n# Stripe Configuration\nSTRIPE_API_KEY=sk_test_your_stripe_test_key\nSTRIPE_PUBLIC_KEY=pk_test_your_stripe_public_test_key  # Optional, for frontend\n\n# SendGrid Configuration\nSENDGRID_API_KEY=your_sendgrid_api_key\nSENDGRID_DEFAULT_FROM=your_email@example.com  # Optional, default sender email\n\n# Application Configuration (Optional)\nDEBUG=True  # Set to False in production\nFLASK_ENV=development  # Set to 'production' in production\n```\n\n### Important Security Notes:\n1. **Never commit your `.env` file** to version control. Add it to your `.gitignore` file.\n2. **Use different keys for development and production**.\n3. **For production**:\n   - Use a strong, randomly generated `SECRET_KEY` (you can generate one with `openssl rand -hex 32`)\n   - Use production database credentials\n   - Use production API keys (not test keys)\n   - Set `DEBUG=False` and `FLASK_ENV=production`\n4. Consider using a secrets management service for production environments.\n\n### Example `.gitignore` entry:\n```\n# Environment files\n.env\n.env.local\n.env*.local\n```",
